# Everything listed here is excluded from the build context, which is what
# `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile copies into the image.
#
# Without this file the image would carry secrets and a host virtualenv, and
# neither problem announces itself — the build succeeds either way.

# Secrets. An image is a distributable artifact: a .env baked into a layer stays
# readable to anyone who pulls the image, and `docker history` will show it.
# Configuration reaches the container through environment variables instead —
# see the `environment:` keys in docker-compose.yml.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*-service-account*.json
credentials.json

# A virtualenv built on the host is the wrong platform with the wrong absolute
# paths, so it is dead weight in a Linux image — and because it sits in the same
# COPY layer as the source, touching it rebuilds everything below.
.venv/
venv/

# Caches. Host .pyc files are never the ones the container would generate.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Scans written at runtime by STORAGE_BACKEND=local. Runtime data, not source,
# and on most hosts this directory is ephemeral anyway.
var/

# Optional engines are installed from their own requirements files, or not at
# all. The Dockerfile deliberately installs only requirements.txt.
requirements-paddle.txt
requirements-crnn.txt

# Kept deliberately: models/ is loaded at runtime when OCR_ENGINE=crnn, and
# fixtures/ is what the test suite and the demo page are built from.
